{{#extend "layout" showSource=true}}

  {{#content "main"}}
  <section class="hero">
    <p class="eyebrow">Recipe &middot; the exit code</p>
    <h1>Await complete(), and catch it</h1>
    <p>
      This is the migration that costs the most if it is missed, because nothing about it is
      visible: the build still ends, the script still exits, and the deploy still runs.
    </p>
  </section>

  <div class="grid">
    <article class="card">
      <h3>The v1 idiom</h3>
      <p>
        A chain that ends at <code>generate()</code>, or a callback-style
        <code>complete(function () { … })</code> with no <code>.catch</code>. v1 logged a
        page's failure and resolved anyway, so a script shaped like that exited 0 on a build
        with pages missing from it.
      </p>
    </article>
    <article class="card">
      <h3>What v2 does</h3>
      <p>
        <code>complete()</code> rejects with an <code>AggregateError</code>, and
        <code>err.failures</code> lists every failed page as
        <code>&#123; view, buildTo, error &#125;</code>. Unhandled, that is now an unhandled
        rejection rather than a silent success.
      </p>
    </article>
    <article class="card">
      <h3>The catch has to do the work too</h3>
      <p>
        A failed build never runs <code>complete()</code>'s own callback. Anything that lived
        there — a sitemap, an index, a deploy step — has to run again in the
        <code>catch</code>, or it quietly stops happening on exactly the builds you most want
        to know about.
      </p>
    </article>
  </div>

  <div class="callout">
    <h2>The shape this script uses</h2>
    <pre><code>const finish = (outcome) =&gt;
  console.log(`post-build step ran after a ${outcome} build`)

await kiss
  .complete(() =&gt; finish('successful'))
  .catch((err) =&gt; {
    reportBuildFailure(err)
    finish('failed')
  })</code></pre>
    <p class="meta">
      <code>reportBuildFailure</code> lives in <code>examples/_shared/site.js</code>: it prints
      each failure and sets <code>process.exitCode = 1</code>. Example 8 is the same shape with
      a feed that really does fail.
    </p>
  </div>
  {{/content}}

  {{#content "demo"}}
  <p>
    Run this example and the console prints
    <code>post-build step ran after a successful build</code>. Run example 8 and the same line
    reads <code>failed</code>, after the failure report — that is the whole recipe: one
    function, called from both branches, so the exit code is the only difference.
  </p>
  {{/content}}

{{/extend}}
